{"cuisine/parsi-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/parsi-cuisine","n":"Parsi cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Parsi cuisine refers to the traditional cuisine of the Parsi people who had migrated into the Indian subcontinent from Persia, and currently spread across the modern-day South Asian republics of India and Pakistan."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"parsi-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parsi_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"ParsiCuisine.com","ref":"site/parsicuisine-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"parsicuisine.com","note":"Long-running community recipe site devoted to Parsi Zoroastrian cooking.","url":"https://www.parsicuisine.com/parsicuisine-com/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Parsi: From Persia to Bombay: Recipes & Tales from the Ancient Culture","ref":"cookbook/parsi-from-persia-to-bombay-recipes-tales-from-the-ancient-culture","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Farokh Talati","note":"Parsi home recipes and history by a London-based chef, published by Bloomsbury.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781472988690","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Dhansak","dish/dhansak"],["Pathia","dish/pathia"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cumin seed","ingredient/cumin-seed"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Gourd","ingredient/gourd"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Lentil","ingredient/lentil"],["Pumpkin","ingredient/pumpkin"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Lime (fruit)","ingredient/lime-fruit"],["Tamarind","ingredient/tamarind"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/latvian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/latvian-cuisine","n":"Latvian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Latvia","intro":[["Latvian cuisine typically consists of agricultural products, with meat featuring in most main dishes. Fish is commonly consumed due to Latvia's location on the eastern shore of the Baltic Sea."],["Latvian cuisine has been influenced by other countries of the Baltic rim. Common ingredients in Latvian recipes are found locally, such as potatoes, wheat, barley, cabbage, onions, eggs and pork. Latvian cuisine is markedly seasonal, and every season has its own distinctive products and dishes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"latvian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Latvian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Latvian Eats","ref":"site/latvianeats-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"latvianeats.com","note":"English-language blog of traditional Latvian recipes, including a project cooking through old Latvian cookbooks.","url":"https://latvianeats.com","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Baltic: New and Old Recipes from Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania","ref":"cookbook/baltic-new-and-old-recipes-from-estonia-latvia-and-lithuania","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Simon Bajada","note":"Survey of traditional and modern cooking across the three Baltic states.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":6,"links":[["Aspic","dish/aspic"],["Blodpalt","dish/blodpalt"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Cold beet soup","dish/cold-beet-soup"],["Kvass","dish/kvass"],["Sklandrausis","dish/sklandrausis"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Rye flour","ingredient/rye-flour"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Blood","ingredient/blood"],["Blood as food","ingredient/blood-as-food"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Beetroot","ingredient/beetroot"],["Kefir","ingredient/kefir"],["Kvass","ingredient/kvass"],["Sorrel","ingredient/sorrel"],["Malt","ingredient/malt"],["Rye bread","ingredient/rye-bread"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Caraway","ingredient/caraway"],["Caraway seed","ingredient/caraway-seed"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Rye","ingredient/rye"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"More from Latvia","count":1,"links":[["Livonian cuisine","cuisine/livonian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine"},"cuisine/cuisine-of-minorca":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-minorca","n":"Minorcan cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Spain","intro":[["Menorcan cuisine refers to the typical food and drink of Menorca."],["Menorca is a rocky island in the Balearic archipelago in Spain, consisting of eight municipalities. Featuring a Mediterranean climate, the weather is milder in the south while in the north there are strong winds all year round. Marine salt, carried by the wind to the pastures where cows graze, is what gives the cheese its typical flavour. Seafood is a major element of the island's cuisine, but additionally, there are horses, pigs (used for cold cuts) and cows (the skin of which is used to produce leather, and the milk to produce cheese). Agriculture is small-scale and varied, consisting of typical Mediterranean products. Within this typical Mediterranean cuisine there are also the influences of various invading people, particularly the English, who brought plum cake, puddings, and punch. The rural and marine cuisine is mostly based on greens and vegetables from one's own garden, locally produced meat, and fish and seafood caught in the same day. Olive oil, although not produced on the island, is also a fundamental ingredient in local dishes."],["Menorcan cuisine is generally simple and seasonal. It is based on fishing, particularly longline fishing, and on seafood, especially crustaceans, clams and squid. Fruits and vegetables are cultivated in as much variety as possible, and on a small scale, only for local consumption."],["Until the second half of the twentieth century, goats were in such abundance that they were only eaten when there was a famine, caused by spoiled crops or insufficient fishing. Today, they are a luxury. Rabbit is another common element of the cuisine. In the seventeenth century, the English unsuccessfully attempted to introduce deer and hares. Few are left nowadays, but rabbits and various fowl are still hunted or bred."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-minorca"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Minorca"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine"},"cuisine/taino-heritage-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/taino-heritage-foodways","n":"Taíno heritage foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Puerto Rico, Cuba, the Dominican Republic and Jamaica","names":"Taíno","intro":[["Spanish officials declared the Taíno extinct by the 1550s, but their culture survived, and today people in Puerto Rico, Cuba, the Dominican Republic and Jamaica who identify as Taíno say they are still here. Taíno words for foods and farming live on in Caribbean speech, and yuca, ",["Cassava","ingredient/cassava"],", their staple, is still eaten, not least as a survival food when hurricanes hit."],[["Casabe","dish/casabe"],", a round bread of grated, dried cassava baked on a griddle, is still a daily food in much of the region, and in 2024 UNESCO recognized cassava bread from Cuba, the Dominican Republic, Haiti, Honduras and Venezuela, with its Indigenous and African roots. In Puerto Rico, Taíno tubers and ",["Achiote","ingredient/annatto"]," season pasteles, and the conuco, a small farm plot, helped rural families feed themselves into the 1900s. Some young Puerto Rican farmers and chefs see these practices as a way back to Taíno foods."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Smithsonian, Caribbean Indigenous Resistance, Taíno Vive","url":"https://my.si.edu/exhibitions/caribbean-indigenous-resistance--resistencia-indgena-del-caribe-tano-vive"},{"name":"UNESCO, Traditional knowledge and practices for the making and consumption of cassava bread","url":"https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/traditional-knowledge-and-practices-for-the-making-and-consumption-of-cassava-bread-02118"},{"name":"Journal of Agriculture, Food Systems, and Community Development, Decolonizing the Caribbean diet (García Polanco and Rodríguez-Cruz, 2019)","url":"https://foodsystemsjournal.org/index.php/fsj/article/download/745/734/1526"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"taino-heritage-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Casabe","dish/casabe"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cassava","ingredient/cassava"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"ingredient/coriander":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/coriander","n":"Coriander","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 16 dishes across 15 cuisines","intro":[["Coriander (), or Coriandrum sativum, is an annual herb in the family Apiaceae. The leaves are known as cilantro () in the US. Most people perceive the leaves as having a fresh, slightly citrus taste. Due to variations in the gene OR6A2, some people perceive its flavor to be more soaplike or rotten."]],"src":"wikipedia","acti